- op: discuss  # a632
  at: '2026-04-07T10:02:14Z'  # a632
  by: agent  # a632
  actor: jgstern_agent  # a632
  clock: 2  # a632
  nonce: a632  # a632
  message: "ROOT CAUSE confirmed by inspecting deep-20260407-050552 alertmanager hg.json. The 4 InclusiveRange.* spurious edges are NOT a within-file type-hierarchy artifact in the way the title implies. They come from Go struct embedding being modeled as 'extends'.\n\nEvidence: timeinterval.go:80-103 defines InclusiveRange and 4 embedders: WeekdayRange/DayOfMonthRange/MonthRange/YearRange — each embeds InclusiveRange. The Go analyzer correctly creates extends edges (struct.WeekdayRange → struct.InclusiveRange, conf 0.95) for each.\n\nThe type_hierarchy.py linker then iterates parent_to_children from extends edges, takes parent InclusiveRange's methods (MarshalText, MarshalYAML, memberFromString), and for each child looks for 'overrides'. WeekdayRange has its own MarshalText (line 389), MarshalYAML (line 381), memberFromString (line 139). MonthRange has its own memberFromString (line 147). The linker creates dispatches_to edges for each, treating them as polymorphic overrides.\n\nTHE BUG: in Go, struct embedding is NOT virtual dispatch. Calling `var ir *InclusiveRange; ir.MarshalText()` ALWAYS goes to InclusiveRange.MarshalText. There is no path where it lands in WeekdayRange.MarshalText. The 'override' is actually a shadow that only applies when the call site holds a *WeekdayRange. So InclusiveRange.MarshalText → WeekdayRange.MarshalText has no semantic meaning.\n\nThis is language-specific: Java/Python/C# concrete-to-concrete inheritance HAS virtual dispatch and dispatches_to is correct. Go struct embedding does not. C++ requires the 'virtual' keyword.\n\nDESIGN CHOICES (need triage before implementing):\n\n(A) **Add per-language config to type_hierarchy linker.** New flag concrete_extends_dispatches: bool (default True; False for Go). Iterates parent_to_children only when the parent is abstract OR the language allows concrete virtual dispatch. Smallest, most targeted change. Coverage cost: thread the language flag into LinkerContext.\n\n(B) **Rename Go embedding edge type from 'extends' to 'embeds'.** Semantically clean (embedding != inheritance), lets the type_hierarchy linker treat the two differently. But many downstream linkers and the slice algorithm consume 'extends' — a rename has cross-cutting impact.\n\n(C) **At Go analyzer level, track methods explicitly defined on the embedder vs promoted from the embedded type.** When the linker iterates overrides, the explicit methods don't dispatch from the parent (no polymorphism), only the promoted methods do (and they're the same code). Most semantically correct but most invasive — adds metadata to every method.\n\nRECOMMENDATION: option (A). Smallest blast radius, language-aware, doesn't break other consumers. The thread-language work is localized to type_hierarchy.py.\n\nALSO observed but not in scope of this item: the Go analyzer FAILS to create implements edges from WeekdayRange/MonthRange/DayOfMonthRange/YearRange to stringableRange, even though they all satisfy it (via memberFromString defined explicitly + setBegin/setEnd promoted from InclusiveRange). The structural matcher doesn't traverse embedded methods when checking interface satisfaction. This is a SEPARATE bug — should be a new tracker item if confirmed.\n\nThe 5th false positive (URL.UnmarshalYAML → mailDev.UnmarshalYAML) is a different beast — likely the cross-package short-name collision case from INV-zomuk's neighborhood. Not investigated here.\n\nRecommend: keep this item title (false positives) but update it to reflect the embedding root cause. Open follow-up items for (i) the missing implements edges through embedded methods and (ii) the cross-file URL/mailDev case if it's a fresh regression."  # a632
