- op: discuss  # c315
  at: '2026-04-07T18:18:10Z'  # c315
  by: agent  # c315
  actor: jgstern_agent  # c315
  clock: 4  # c315
  nonce: c315  # c315
  message: "ANSWER: Yes — concrete-extends-as-virtual-dispatch is a language-specific assumption that fails for at least 4 languages. The default should be flipped or made explicit per language.\n\nLANGUAGES WHERE 'extends' IMPLIES VIRTUAL DISPATCH (current behavior is correct):\n- Java: all non-final methods are virtual by default\n- Kotlin: methods are final by default but 'open' classes/methods unlock virtual dispatch\n- C#: explicit 'virtual'/'override' keywords; 'extends' ≠ guaranteed virtual without those keywords\n- Python: all methods are virtual (duck-typed)\n- Scala: similar to Kotlin (final by default with override keyword)\n- Ruby: all methods are virtual\n\nLANGUAGES WHERE 'extends' DOES NOT IMPLY VIRTUAL DISPATCH (false positives):\n- **Go**: struct embedding is composition, not inheritance — calling ir.Method() on the parent type goes to the parent's method, never the embedder's. This is the case WI-sukav describes.\n- **C++**: only methods marked 'virtual' dispatch polymorphically; non-virtual methods are statically resolved at the static type. The Go fix would have an analog: the C++ analyzer would need to mark methods as concrete_extends_dispatches=False unless 'virtual' is present.\n- **Rust**: trait dispatch is the only polymorphism; struct fields/embedded types don't override trait methods virtually — the analyzer should not emit dispatches_to from a struct's inherent impl methods to those of an 'embedded' field. Rust doesn't have a direct equivalent of Go embedding, but the auto-deref for newtype patterns is structurally similar.\n- **Swift**: 'final' methods do not dispatch virtually. Default is virtual (like Kotlin 'open'-by-default) — actually Swift defaults to virtual for class methods but 'final' suppresses it.\n- **C# nuance**: when neither 'virtual' nor 'override' is present, method resolution is static. The C# analyzer should track whether a method is marked virtual.\n\nLANGUAGE-INDEPENDENT IMPLEMENTATION:\n\nTwo equally valid framings of option (A):\n\n(A1) Per-language default flag in type_hierarchy linker:\n  default_concrete_extends_dispatches = {Java: True, Kotlin: True, C#: False, Python: True, Ruby: True, Go: False, C++: False, Rust: False, Swift: True, Scala: True, Dart: True, ...}\n  This is a simple table lookup driven by edge.language at link time.\n\n(A2) Per-edge flag at the analyzer level:\n  Each analyzer emits 'extends' edges with meta.virtual_dispatch: True/False. The Go analyzer always sets False; the C++/C# analyzers compute it from the 'virtual' keyword presence; Java/Python/Kotlin always set True. The type_hierarchy linker reads the edge meta and gates dispatches_to creation accordingly.\n\nA2 is cleaner because it allows per-method overrides (e.g., a C++ class with both virtual and non-virtual methods). A1 is faster to implement and covers ~95% of cases. Recommend A1 for the immediate fix and a separate WI for A2 once C++ analyzer infrastructure justifies the per-method tracking.\n\nCROSS-LINKER CHECK: same gap likely exists in any other linker that walks parent_to_children from extends edges and infers polymorphism. Quick audit:\n- type_hierarchy.py — confirmed (this WI)\n- supply_chain.py — operates on contains/imports, not extends, so unaffected\n- centrality.py — uses dispatches_to as input, so it inherits the type_hierarchy bug; fixing that linker fixes the downstream symptom\n- slice.py — same: consumes dispatches_to\n- io_boundary.py — operates on call edges directly, not affected\n\nSo the fix is localized to type_hierarchy.py with the per-language flag table. Side note: this is the same architectural pattern as the dataflow_patterns YAMLs — language-specific behavioral differences captured in a small per-language config rather than hardcoded in cross-cutting code.\n\nShould I implement option (A1) with the flag table, or do you want option (A2) for cleaner per-method semantics? My recommendation is (A1) now for the immediate alertmanager fix, with (A2) as a future WI."  # c315
